faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
Neither r6 nor r7 has crashed, and there is no active network fault. Both devices are running all FRR daemons (zebra, ospfd, mgmtd, staticd, watchfrr) continuously since lab startup at 12:03 with zero process restarts, zero watchfrr recovery events, and zero error messages in their FRR logs. All OSPF interfaces on both routers are UP with standard Hello 10 s / Dead 40 s timers, and every adjacency is in the Full/DR or Full/BDR state: r6 holds three Full adjacencies (r4, r5, r7) and r7 holds one Full adjacency (r6). The multi-area OSPF design — Area 0 anchored at r3, Area 1 spanning r4–r8, Area 2 on r8's stub interfaces, and an Area 1 virtual-link between r4 and r8 — is consistently configured and fully converged. Routing tables on all sampled routers contain the complete expected prefix set, and the end-to-end pc0↔pc12 ping returns 0% packet loss. The "alert that cleared itself" was almost certainly a transient OSPF convergence event: immediately after the lab started, OSPF adjacencies took roughly 40 seconds to reach Full state (the standard dead-interval), during which time any traffic destined for the Area 1 cluster would have been silently dropped, producing exactly the "everything going through the middle is falling into a hole" symptom the ops team observed. Once OSPF converged the alert auto-cleared. No corrective action is required; the root cause was normal OSPF startup convergence latency, not a device crash or misconfiguration.
=== END ===